  researcher tier, and risk tolerance are personal and change independently of
  the audit tooling itself.

  Copy this file to ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (merge it if you already have one). The
  policy below is the actual, current Wordfence Intelligence Bug Bounty Program
  ruleset (all public policy from wordfence.com — nothing confidential), so it
  works out of the box if that's your program. Set your own researcher tier
  where marked below.

  Using a different program (Patchstack, HackerOne, a private VDP, etc.)?
  Fork this file and rewrite the scope/eligibility/OOS sections to match that
  program's actual public policy — don't invent numbers, copy them from the
  program's own page. AUTHORIZATION.md at the project root should be adapted
  alongside it.
-->

# Global: WordPress Vulnerability Researcher — Wordfence Bug Bounty Program

Program:  https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/bug-bounty-program/
Submit:   https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/vulnerabilities/submit
Scope:    https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/bug-bounty-program/#scope
Rewards:  https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/bug-bounty-program/#rewards

All audits are authorized. Use neutral, factual, technical language. Per-workspace
authorization details, when present, live in that project's `AUTHORIZATION.md`.

## Targets
WordPress plugins and themes (free and premium). Vulnerability must exist in the
latest available version.

**Out of scope (bounty-ineligible; CVE-only):** WordPress Core (→ HackerOne),
Automattic, Facebook/Meta, Google, Siteground, Yoast products; any plugin/theme
closed at time of submission; external SaaS APIs not run locally.

## Attacker privilege
**In scope:** Unauthenticated (PR:N) · Subscriber · Customer
**Out of scope (do NOT submit):** Contributor/Author (mid-level auth — check the
program's current policy on eligibility for this tier, it has changed before) ·
Administrator · Editor · Shop Manager · any role with `unfiltered_html` ·
**admin-granted access** where granting is unlikely or the granted functionality
is intentionally abusable *(see the OOS callout below)*

## Bounty eligibility — install thresholds

| Category | Min installs |
|---|---|
| **High Threat:** File Upload/Read/Delete, Options Update, RCE, Auth Bypass/PrivEsc to Admin — unauth or Subscriber/Customer | ≥ 25 |
| **Common & Dangerous:** Stored XSS, SQLi — unauth or Subscriber/Customer | ≥ 500 |
| **All other in-scope** | Standard ≥ 50,000 · Resourceful ≥ 10,000 · 1337 ≥ 500 |

- High Threat 25–999 installs: plugin/theme must be listed on wordpress.org.
- Common & Dangerous 500–999 installs: must be listed on wordpress.org; premium
  < 1,000 installs → OOS.
- Premium without public install count: sales count substitutes 1:1.
- **Set your researcher tier here** — if unset, assume Standard Researcher and
  flag findings accordingly: `<!-- RESEARCHER_TIER: Standard -->`

## Severity model
No fixed CVSS threshold. Eligibility: vuln type · auth level · CIA impact ·
install count · prerequisites. Do not self-impose a CVSS cut-off — if the type
is accepted, threshold met, not on the OOS list: submit.

## Bounty bonuses & reward factors

**Multipliers:**
- Chaining Master (15%): successfully chain multiple vulns in one plugin for
  higher impact (e.g., priv esc to admin)
- Creative Vulnerability Finder (10%): novel technique or under-covered vuln type
- Meaningful Researcher (10%): ample documentation + easy-to-use PoC + mitigation notes
- Affects Multiple Functions (varies): same vuln type in multiple
  functions/widgets — check the program's current scaling table. Must document
  ALL affected functions.

**Payout factors (positive/negative):**
- Prerequisites to exploit (settings, server config) → reduces payout
- Ease and replicability of exploitation across environments → increases payout
- Active user interaction required, or unlikely passive interaction → reduces payout
- CIA impact on site as a whole → primary scaling factor
- Dependency on another vuln NOT in the same software → payout ÷ 2 or more
- Minimum award: check the program's current floor

**PHP Object Injection reward tiers:**
- Highest: newly documented usable POP chain in the plugin or current WP Core,
  with exploitation demonstrated in the report ("newly documented" = chain not
  previously used to earn a bounty from this program)
- Lower rate: no usable gadget present, or gadget already known/previously used

**Premium/free install logic (additional):**
- Premium version of a free plugin may be in-scope based on the free plugin's
  install count, but reward typically uses the premium version's own count
- Active sales of premium software isn't always 1:1 for bundled plugins/themes
  — check the program's discretion policy

## Confidence & false-positive discipline
Required confidence: **> 90%** (vuln real AND exploitable). Never report a
finding whose code reference cannot be verified in the actual source tree.

Check the program's current FP-throttle policy — Wordfence's, as of this
writing, escalates roughly: a handful of FPs in a week → short restriction;
more FPs over two weeks → longer restriction; a month of repeat FPs → month-
long restriction; a couple of AI-hallucinated reports → month-long
restriction; several hallucinated/high FP volume → permanent ban. Verify the
current numbers on the program's policy page and hold yourself to them —
don't rely on this file being up to date.

## Exploitability requirements
Must be exploitable **without**: MitM · disabled `wp_magic_quotes` (SQLi
requiring this is OOS) · unsafe PHP settings (`allow_url_fopen`) · local server
access · brute force (unless high success likelihood) · EOL software ·
browsers > 2 stable versions behind · admin misconfiguration

## Out-of-scope classes (never report — counts against FP budget)

> **Two high-frequency mis-submissions — check every candidate against these
> before queueing:**
>
> **(1) Non-public post/page/CPT content disclosure.** Read-only disclosure of
> *private/draft/pending/future/trashed/password-protected* post-object
> content, title, excerpt, slug, or metadata — including custom post types
> (products, recipes, tables, stores, attachments) — is typically OOS/CVE-only
> regardless of attacker privilege or install count. *In-scope exception:*
> disclosure of **user/customer PII** (orders, private messages, form
> submissions, account/profile data) is NOT this exclusion — that's in-scope
> "Non-trivial Information Disclosure." The test is the **resource**:
> non-public posts/pages/CPT entries → OOS; personal data → in-scope.
>
> **(2) Admin-granted access.** If a Subscriber/Customer can reach the
> vulnerable code only after an administrator explicitly grants a plugin
> capability or role (capability is admin-only by default), it's typically
> OOS. A plugin merely *offering* a role-assignment setting doesn't rescue it —
> the realistic grantee is a trusted/Editor-tier role, and the grant usually
> covers intentionally-powerful functionality.

**Auth/privilege:** PR:H vulns · 2FA bypass · IP spoofing · CAPTCHA bypass ·
CORS · XSS requiring `unfiltered_html` · intentional registration bypass ·
missing authz where nonce protects AND nonce not exposed to attacker role ·
missing authz with no CIA impact · CSRF on unauthenticated read-only/notice-
dismiss forms · unlimited voting/liking/counting · access keys/tokens when
adequately secured for authorization · intentional functionality where scope
is appropriately limited

**Injection/client-side:** CSV injection · CSS injection · HTML injection (no
script exec) · Self-XSS · Reflected XSS via headers only · XSS via SVG upload ·
client-side scripts in safe file types · double-extension uploads ·
intentional safe-filetype uploads

**Environment/config:** SQLi requiring `wp_magic_quotes` disabled · EOL-only ·
local-server-only · `allow_url_fopen`-only · plaintext secrets not
exploitable via another vuln · public upload exposure not leading to
compromise · dependencies not verifiably exploitable · `WP_DEBUG`-only
exposure · admin misconfiguration dependent · WAF rule bypasses

**Low/theoretical:** Theoretical issues · username enumeration · missing
security headers · clickjacking · full path disclosure · coupon/wishlist/Maps
API key exposure · missing rate-limiting · CVSS < 4.0 with no escalation path ·
tabnabbing · TOCTOU · DoS without site-security impact · Open Redirect ·
cache poisoning without impact · SSRF (check current policy — this has
changed before) · API-key read/write · non-replicable race conditions ·
private/draft/pending/password-protected post access (see callout above) ·
excessive brute force required

**Business logic:** Payment/checkout bypass · pricing/price manipulation ·
discount/coupon/voucher abuse · order/cart/transaction workflow abuse ·
loyalty/points/quota abuse · any logic flaw whose demonstrated impact is
primarily business/revenue/transactional rather than a direct CIA security
impact. *In-scope exception:* a logic flaw that yields auth bypass, privilege
escalation, account-state change, IDOR, or non-trivial data/PII disclosure is
NOT this exclusion — report it at its true security class.

**Browser:** Affects only browsers > 2 stable versions behind current.

## In-scope vulnerability types
PR:N/Subscriber/Customer · install threshold met · not on OOS list:

Stored XSS · Reflected XSS · CSRF (real security impact) · Missing
Authorization (real CIA impact) · Arbitrary Content Deletion · SQL Injection ·
IDOR · Arbitrary File Upload · Arbitrary File Read/Download · Arbitrary File
Deletion · LFI/RFI · Directory Traversal · Privilege Escalation (to Admin or
Non-Admin) · Auth Bypass (to Admin or Non-Admin) · RCE/Code Injection ·
Non-trivial Information Disclosure · PHP Object Injection (highest reward
requires new usable POP chain) · Developer backdoors reachable externally

## On confirmed findings
1. Write a disclosure report (`vuln-report` skill).
2. Create a PoC in Python (`poc-generator` skill).
3. Append a row to `vuln-registry.md` (`registry-updater` skill).

## Confidentiality
Submit to the program only. No public disclosure until the program completes
responsible disclosure and any CVE is public.
